          "path": "_m/g/c/q/0/hereford-cathedral-wp/hereford-cathedral-eng-4.jpg",
          "filename": "Hereford cathedral 024.JPG",
          "caption": "Hereford Cathedral, England. This is Thomas Cantilupe's tomb, according to this page. From Fisher, A. Hugh, The Cathedral Church of Hereford, London, 1898, p.37[1]: \"The North Transept. Passing through the north arch of the tower we come into some of the most interesting parts of the Cathedral. The transept beyond was entirely rebuilt for the reception of the shrine of Bishop Cantilupe, when his body was removed from the Lady Chapel in 1287, after the miracles reported at his tomb had already largely increased the revenues of the Cathedral. The unusual shape of the arches and the fine and effective windows of this transept render it one of the most distinguished English specimens of the style\".",
          "alt": "Hereford Cathedral, England. This is Thomas Cantilupe's tomb, according to this page. From Fisher, A. Hugh, The Cathedral Church of Hereford, London, 1898, p.37[1]: \"The North Transept. Passing through the north arch of the tower we come into some of the most interesting parts of the Cathedral. The transept beyond was entirely rebuilt for the reception of the shrine of Bishop Cantilupe, when his body was removed from the Lady Chapel in 1287, after the miracles reported at his tomb had already largely increased the revenues of the Cathedral. The unusual shape of the arches and the fine and effective windows of this transept render it one of the most distinguished English specimens of the style\".",
          "width": 1200,
          "height": 900,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
